Leadership – How: Nodir
This year, I gave Nodir the opportunity to step up from delivering individual engineering tasks to taking ownership of the DiP workstream, with Oyin working alongside him. This was an important opportunity for him to demonstrate his technical leadership and ability to guide another engineer while driving a wider initiative.
I appreciate the work Nodir has put into DiP and the progress made in bringing it to the developer community. This is a positive achievement and demonstrates his technical capability and commitment to the workstream.
However, I believe Nodir has further opportunities to develop his leadership approach, particularly in the following areas:
	•	Communication and visibility: Nodir tends to work independently and can be quite reserved when sharing progress. I often need to reach out to understand what is happening, where we are against the roadmap and whether Oyin is progressing in the right direction. As a workstream lead, he needs to provide regular updates without waiting to be asked. This does not mean arranging daily meetings, but a concise weekly update covering progress, upcoming priorities, blockers and support required would make a significant difference.
	•	Roadmap ownership and accountability: Leading a workstream means providing direction to the engineers working with you. DiP had ambitious objectives this year, and I would have liked to see stronger ownership of the roadmap, with clearer milestones and delivery expectations. Where priorities change or blockers prevent delivery, I expect Nodir to raise these early, explain the impact and propose alternatives rather than allowing stories to carry over between sprints or objectives to roll into the next quarter without reassessment.
	•	Delegation and driving dependencies: I would like Nodir to become more effective in identifying what he needs to own himself and what can be delegated to others, including the PO and SRE team. Not every task requires extensive investigation or a formal meeting. Sometimes a clear email or direct conversation with the relevant team is enough to get an action moving. For example, the SRE dashboard dependency for DiP needs clear ownership, agreed actions and follow-up rather than still remain an outstanding without any clear ETA.
I would also strongly encourage Nodir to spend more time developing his knowledge of the Gen-AI space, particularly the training and tools available within Citi. I believe there is a good opportunity for him to use these capabilities to take DiP further and improve the experience we provide to our developer community. More broadly, making better use of Gen-AI tools in his day-to-day work could help improve his productivity and the way he approaches engineering tasks. Taking the initiative with his own development in this area will become increasingly important.
I recognise that leading a workstream for the first time is a learning experience, and I appreciate the effort Nodir has made this year. However, the next step is to build the consistency and confidence required to lead a workstream end to end. Developing these areas will help me build greater confidence in delegating wider responsibilities to him, while allowing him to increase his impact across the Container Security portfolio.
